NAME
       sha256sum - compute and check SHA-256 checksums

SYNOPSIS
       sha256sum [FILE...]
       sha256sum -c [--quiet] [--status] CHECKSUM-FILE
       sha1sum, sha512sum: the same with SHA-1 and SHA-512

DESCRIPTION
       sha256sum reads each FILE (or standard input) and prints its SHA-256
       checksum (64 hexadecimal digits), two spaces and the file name. Any
       change to a file changes its checksum, so comparing checksums tells
       whether a file was copied or downloaded intact.

       sha1sum and sha512sum work the same way with other algorithms.
       SHA-256 and SHA-512 are the safe choices today; SHA-1 and MD5 are
       only good for spotting accidental damage.

OPTIONS
       -c, --check
              Read lines of checksums and names from the file and check each
              file: it prints NAME: OK or NAME: FAILED.

       --quiet
              With -c, print only the failures.

       --status
              With -c, print nothing; the exit status says whether all files
              matched (0) or not (1).

       -b, -t Read in binary or text mode (the same on Linux).

EXAMPLES
       Save checksums of some files and check them later:

           sha256sum /etc/passwd /etc/group > /root/sums.txt
           sha256sum -c /root/sums.txt

       Compare a copy with the original:

           sha256sum /root/backup.tar.gz
           ssh root@server2 sha256sum /tmp/backup.tar.gz

SEE ALSO
       md5sum(1), b2sum(1), cksum(1)
